   });
 }
+
+/**
+ * The natural pixel size of a file the customer has just chosen, before it is
+ * uploaded — so a background that cannot print can be refused with the file
+ * still in their hand, instead of after a round trip to storage.
+ *
+ * `naturalWidth`/`naturalHeight` are already EXIF-oriented in every current
+ * browser (`image-orientation: from-image` is the default), so a phone photo
+ * measures the way it will be seen. Resolves `null` when the browser cannot
+ * decode the file; the caller decides whether that is a refusal.
+ */
+export function readFileNaturalSize(file: File): Promise<{ width: number; height: number } | null> {
+  return new Promise((resolve) => {
+    const url = URL.createObjectURL(file);
+    const img = new window.Image();
+    img.onload = () => {
+      URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
+      resolve(
+        img.naturalWidth > 0 && img.naturalHeight > 0
+          ? { width: img.naturalWidth, height: img.naturalHeight }
+          : null,
+      );
+    };
+    img.onerror = () => {
+      URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
+      resolve(null);
+    };
+    img.src = url;
+  });
+}
diff --git a/docs/card-artwork-print-integrity.md b/docs/card-artwork-print-integrity.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9742588
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/card-artwork-print-integrity.md
@@ -0,0 +1,164 @@
+# Artwork that cannot print is not allowed onto a card
+
+The third document about the same subject, and the one that changes the rule.
+`card-artwork-crop-plan.md` made the loss measurable. `card-artwork-shape-plan.md`
+found that the loss was one wrong export preset applied 207 times, and said the
+fix is the artwork, not the software. Both left every gate advisory except the
+catalog sync's, and that one is off.
+
+This plan closes the door. It is the outcome of a full read of every path a
+piece of artwork takes from a file on someone's desk to a sheet of A6 card, and
+of one decision taken on the back of it: **artwork of the wrong shape or too few
+pixels is refused at the door, not reported afterwards.**
+
+## What the read found
+
+Every renderer agrees, and every renderer is correct. `coverCrop` (browser),
+pdfkit `cover` (PDF) and the crop maths (`card-crop.ts`) are the same rule; the
+preview and the print differ by 0.06mm. The cropping in the field is not a
+rendering fault. It is artwork that is the wrong shape, drawn correctly.
+
+Beyond that, four things the previous two plans did not cover:
+
+1. **"Upload your own artwork" was broken in two ways at once.**
+   `designs-client.tsx` built its document with a **450 × 600 image element** on
+   a canvas that has been **450 × 634** since ADR 0161 — the same regression the
+   catalog builder was fixed for, in a file that was not. Every custom upload got
+   an 8mm white strip along the bottom of the front, and because an image
+   element is _stretched_ to its box on every renderer, the artwork was forced
+   into 3:4 whatever shape it was. An A6-correct 1240 × 1748 upload was squashed
+   5.7%. Nothing measured it, because no distortion check existed.
+2. **Nothing enforced anything on a customer's background.** The upload goes
+   browser → Supabase on a signed URL; the API never sees the bytes, and the
+   only server-side check on a document (`parseDocument`) is the reserved
+   footer. The editor's crop note, the ops pre-flight and the low-DPI warnings
+   are all advisory, and `DesignAsset.width/height` are whatever the browser
+   said.
+3. **EXIF orientation was measured wrong.** A phone photo is often stored
+   landscape with a tag saying "rotate". The browser and pdfkit honour the tag;
+   `sharp.metadata()` reports the un-rotated header size. The catalog sync
+   measures with the header, so a rotated portrait photo of exactly the right
+   shape reads as landscape and "47% cropped".
+4. **Image elements can be distorted silently.** The aspect lock (ADR 0069) is
+   a default, not a rule: a lock switched off, a legacy design, or the flow in
+   (1) stretches an image and nothing in the editor, the pre-send check or the
+   print run says so.
+
+## Decisions
+
+**D1 — A background must be the right shape and carry enough pixels.** One
+verdict, `backgroundArtworkVerdict` in `shared-types/card-artwork.ts`, derived
+from the two pre-flights beside it: shape passes when `cropVerdict` is `ok`
+(≤2% of an axis, ~1.5mm per edge on A6 — forgives a true-A6 export against the
+634-unit canvas), pixels pass when `printDpiVerdict` is not `low` (200dpi:
+827 × 1165 on A6). The catalog sync's gate uses the same crop rule, so our
+artwork and the customer's are judged alike.
+
+**D2 — Refuse twice: before the upload, and at the save.** The browser measures
+the chosen file (`readFileNaturalSize`) and refuses before a byte is uploaded,
+with the message naming the size to export at. The API applies the identical
+verdict in `SavedDesignsService.parseDocument` via `ArtworkGateService` — the
+choke point ADR 0171 chose, for the same reasons: every downstream route is
+closed at once, and it fails with the design open in front of the customer.
+
+**D3 — The gate is on the door, not the room.** Only a background that is _new
+to the design_ is measured (`newMemberBackgroundUrls`). A background the design
+already carried was accepted once; refusing it on the next save would trap a
+customer in a design they can neither fix nor keep. Catalog artwork is skipped —
+it is measured at the sync, and most of it would fail here until the re-export
+lands, which would stop a customer saving a text edit on artwork they cannot
+change.
+
+**D4 — Fail open on infrastructure, closed on artwork.** The server-side check
+needs one fetch of the file header from storage. If storage is unreachable the
+save goes ahead with a warning in the log; the pre-upload check has already had
+its say, and a storage blip must not stop every customer saving every design. A
+file that _is_ measured and is wrong is refused.
+
+**D5 — Elements are warned, not refused.** ADR 0162's reasoning stands for
+image elements: a small logo is a legitimate element, and an element never loses
+its edges. What it can lose is its proportions, so `elementDistortion` measures
+that and the editor says so, with a one-click "Restore proportions". Making
+distortion a save-time refusal is deliberately not done: a customer who
+switches the lock off and stretches a texture has done it on purpose.
+
+**D6 — Custom artwork is a background.** `customArtworkDocument` replaces the
+450 × 600 element with a full-bleed front background — undistorted, locked under
+the customer's text, and measured by D1 like everything else.
+
+**D7 — Orientation is applied before anything is judged.** `orientedPixelSize`
+swaps the axes for EXIF 5–8. The gate uses it; the catalog sync should (Phase 3).
+
+## What shipped in this change
+
+- `shared-types/card-artwork.ts`: `backgroundArtworkVerdict`,
+  `orientedPixelSize`, `elementDistortion`, `customArtworkDocument`,
+  `newMemberBackgroundUrls`. Pure, tested in `apps/api/src/common/card-artwork.spec.ts`
+  against the real cases (the 2:3 export, the A6 fix, the rotated photo, the
+  450 × 600 squash).
+- `apps/api/src/saved-designs/artwork-gate.service.ts`, wired into
+  `SavedDesignsService.create` (both branches) and `update` (with the stored
+  document as `previous`). Tested with fixture bytes, including SSRF and
+  fail-open.
+- `designs-client.tsx`: measures before uploading, refuses with the export
+  size, builds the document from `customArtworkDocument`.
+- `design-editor-client.tsx`: the same pre-upload refusal for a page
+  background; a distortion warning and "Restore proportions" for a selected
+  image element.
+- `lib/image-natural-size.ts`: `readFileNaturalSize`.
+
+## Phases still to do
+
+Each its own PR, merged when green before the next.
+
+### Phase 1 — Re-export the catalog and close its gate
+
+Re-export the 207 designs at **1748 × 2480** (A5 at 300dpi; the same shape as
+A6 and enough pixels for either size), re-attach in Airtable, run the sync,
+confirm `cropped` is empty in the sync summary, then switch
+`catalog_reject_cropped_artwork` on. This is the fix for the card in the
+screenshot; nothing in software removes that crop.
+
+### Phase 2 — Calibrate the borderless printer
+
+A borderless office printer reaches the edge by _enlarging_ the page a few
+per cent and cropping the overhang, and the amount is a driver setting. Print
+`kudos-borderless-calibration.pdf` (one A6 page, one A5) on the real stock and
+read the loss per edge off the rulers. Under ~1mm: the exact-trim PDF is right
+as it is. Over: add an `overscanMm` option to `faceGeometry` (page stays trim
+size; the design is scaled into trim − 2·overscan; the background covers the
+page) and an admin setting to carry the measured number, so what the printer
+throws away is bleed rather than card.
+
+### Phase 3 — Orientation in the catalog sync
+
+`copyImage` measures with `sharp.metadata()`; pass the result through
+`orientedPixelSize` so a rotated attachment is judged the way it is seen.
+Persist `artworkWidth/Height` oriented.
+
+### Phase 4 — Distortion at the point of sale
+
+A design-level finding in the pre-send check ("an image on the front is
+stretched 6% out of shape"), the same shape as the reserved-footer warning, so a
+legacy design from the old upload flow is caught before payment.
+
+### Phase 5 — Browser print, retired or made honest
+
+`Browser print` insets the card 5mm for a printer that leaves a margin. The
+printer does not, and the PDF is full-bleed at trim, so the two outputs differ
+by 10% in card width. Either remove the button or render it at trim like the
+PDF.
+
+### Phase 6 — Measure at upload, once
+
+`POST /design-assets` records the browser's word for `width/height`. Have the
+API measure the object itself on that call (the gate's `measure`, cached onto
+the row) so the "Your uploads" picker can grey out a background that would be
+refused before the customer picks it.
+
+## What this does not fix
+
+Custom-artwork designs saved by the old flow still carry a 450 × 600 element.
+They render exactly as they did — squashed, with the strip — until the customer
+opens the design and selects the image (the editor now says so and offers the
+one-click fix), or Phase 4 catches it at the point of sale. Cards already printed are printed.
